  <section class="faith-intro">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <details class="faith-intro-disclosure" data-faith-intro>
        <summary class="faith-intro-summary">
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-label" data-faith-intro-label>Read introduction</span>
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faith-intro-body">
          <p class="faith-intro-prose hero-excerpt-dropcap">The Letter to Diognetus is a second- or third-century Christian apology to a pagan inquirer, asking why Christians live as they do. The author - unknown to us - answers with one of the most striking descriptions of the Christian’s place in the world ever written: Christians dwell in the world as the soul dwells in the body, holding it together precisely because they love it without belonging to it. The letter is often anthologized as a foundational document of Christian political theology.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
    
